# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
EN = {
    "hunt_apotheosis": {
        "title": "Apotheosis", "subtitle": "Affixes, gems and gear that never repeats",
        "intro": [
            "Apotheosis rewrites the rules of loot. An ordinary iron sword from a chest may now turn out to be a 'Blazing Blade of Devastation' with three affixes and a gem socket — or it may stay an ordinary iron sword.",
            "The mod offers no progression in the usual sense. It offers a lottery you'll play for the rest of the pack, and the tools to tilt that lottery your way.",
            "It also lifts the enchanting ceiling: level thirty stops being the limit.",
        ],
        "quests": {
            "affixes": {"title": "Affixes", "text": [
                "Dropped gear now has a rarity and affixes: grey common, green uncommon, blue rare, purple epic and orange mythic. A common piece carries one or two properties and no sockets at all; a mythic one carries four properties, two effects, a special ability and up to four sockets.",
                "First thing to understand: the old rule that a diamond sword beats an iron one no longer holds. A mythic iron one hits harder.",
                "You don't throw away the finds you don't want: the salvaging table turns them into a material of the same rarity, from mysterious scrap metal to a godforged pearl. That is the currency for the rest of this chapter."]},
            "gems": {"title": "Gems", "text": [
                "Gems slot into sockets and grant bonuses: damage, speed, protection, luck. Each has six steps of purity, from cracked to perfect, and the gap between the ends of that scale is enormous.",
                "The cutting table raises purity by one step for gem dust and a rarity material. The higher the step, the dearer it gets: flawless to perfect costs nine dust and three godforged pearls.",
                "A gem case holds thirty-two thousand stones of every kind and can cut them inside itself."]},
            "sigils": {"title": "Sigils", "text": [
                "Sigils are applied at a smithing table: the item in the base slot, the sigil in the upgrade slot. The sigil of socketing adds a socket while the item has fewer than two; the sigil of withdrawal pulls every gem back out; the sigil of unnaming strips an ornate name.",
                "The sigil of rebirth never touches the item directly. It is fuel for the reforging table, and the higher the rarity, the more of them a single roll burns. The sigil of enhancement feeds the augmenting table, which raises a chosen affix by a quarter or rerolls that one affix without disturbing the rest.",
                "Two sigils cannot be bought: the sigil of malice drops from bosses, and the sigil of supremacy is the prize for the hundredth wave of an endless gateway."]},
            "reforge": {"title": "Reforging", "text": [
                "The reforging table rolls an item again from scratch, and what decides the outcome is not the table but what you put into it: the rarity material you feed it is the rarity you get back. The simple table covers common through rare; the full one opens epic and mythic.",
                "Three results are offered; the second costs twice as much, the third three times. A mythic roll runs to fifty levels, three materials and five sigils of rebirth, and it can still hand you the wrong thing.",
                "The salvaging table pulls materials and dust out of gear you don't need — that's what funds all this alchemy."]},
            "enchanting": {"title": "Apothic Enchanting", "text": [
                "Apotheosis' table replaces the vanilla one and shows three numbers instead of one. Eterna is the ceiling: whatever you have gathered is the level you can enchant to. Quanta is the spread: the higher it runs, the further the result wanders around the level you picked. Arcana is rarity: it tilts the weights toward expensive enchantments and adds more of them.",
                "Bookshelves stop being interchangeable. Hellshelves give Eterna and Quanta, seashelves Eterna and Arcana, the geode shelf cuts away the negative half of the spread, the sightshelf adds clues, and the beeshelf drops Eterna by thirty to buy a hundred Quanta.",
                "Level thirty comes from fifteen ordinary shelves. Past that, the real building starts."]},
            "infusion": {"title": "Infusion", "text": [
                "At forty-five Eterna the table stops merely enchanting and starts creating. Infusion is a craft performed inside the table itself, checking all three numbers at once.",
                "That is how a hellshelf becomes infused, a seashelf becomes infused, a dormant deepshelf becomes a deepshelf. The same road gives budding amethyst, a real trident out of an inert one, and the extraction tome, which strips enchantments without destroying the item.",
                "Every next recipe demands a stronger table than the last. Infusion is a ladder where each rung builds the one above it."]},
            "deepshelf": {"title": "The Deep", "text": [
                "A dormant deepshelf infused gives seventy. Past that you need an Ancient City: echo shards raise the ceiling to seventy-five, and eighty has exactly one road — kill the Warden for its tendril.",
                "Sculkshelves built on that tendril give ten Eterna each, fifteen Quanta or Arcana, and an extra clue in the preview.",
                "Eighty is the level where vanilla enchantments stop being interesting and the mod's own start showing up: Berserker's Fury, Boon of the Earth, Endless Quiver, Life-Mending."]},
            "hundred": {"title": "The Hundred", "text": [
                "The last twenty levels cost more than everything before them combined. Infused breath, an infusion of dragon's breath at eighty Eterna, opens the endshelves and ninety.",
                "The hundred comes from a single shelf: the draconic endshelf, which wants a dragon head from the outer End. It carries twenty Eterna and removes the ceiling outright.",
                "And even that is not enough: the Eterna ceiling is cut by Apotheosis' world tier. On Haven the table will not pass thirty no matter how many shelves surround it. A real hundred exists only on Pinnacle.",
                "At the top waits the Library of Alexandria, a vault for enchantments up to level thirty-one, built only by infusion at a hundred Eterna and a hundred Arcana."],
                "rewards": ["A table with no ceiling"]},
            "tomes": {"title": "Tomes", "text": [
                "Tomes steer enchanting: the tome of the archers rolls only bow enchantments, the tome of chestplates only chestplate ones. That turns the lottery into a choice.",
                "The other direction is stripping enchantments. The scrap tome takes half and destroys the item, the improved one takes everything and still destroys it, and the extraction tome takes everything and leaves the item whole.",
                "A prismatic web in an anvil removes curses. An ordinary library stores enchantments up to level sixteen and hands them back one level at a time, each level costing twice the last."]},
            "leads": {"title": "Ender Lead", "text": [
                "A flimsy ender lead catches animals, a plain one catches almost anything, and the occult one binds what it caught to a spawner.",
                "The plain lead is infused from the flimsy one; the occult from the plain, but only with a witch already inside it, and only at seventy-five Eterna with eighty-five Quanta.",
                "It is the most direct way to get a spawner for the mob you want rather than the one you were given."]},
            "spawners": {"title": "Spawners", "text": [
                "A spawner can be carried off with a chain and then rebuilt with runes: drop the darkness requirement, remove the player check, speed it up, widen its radius.",
                "That turns a dungeon into a farm, and a farm into a source of experience and affixed gear."]},
            "invaders": {"title": "Invaders", "text": [
                "Now and then an ordinary spawn is replaced by an Apothic Invader: a pillar of light, a sound and a line in the chat. It is a named mob carrying the full stat load of its rarity, and killing it is harder than it looks.",
                "Every invader wears exactly one affixed item, and that item drops guaranteed, with the invader's name on it. On top of that comes a gem or a sigil of malice, the only way to get one.",
                "There are no invaders on Haven at all. They start on the second tier, and the higher the world, the more often they come."]},
            "augment": {"title": "Augmenting Table", "text": [
                "Reforging throws the whole item again; the augmenting table works one affix at a time. A chosen affix rises by a quarter or gets rerolled, and the rest stay exactly as they were.",
                "The price matches. Two sigils of enhancement and experience worth well over two hundred levels for a single improvement. The sigils are mixed on godforged pearls, and those only come from salvaging mythic gear.",
                "This is where the lottery ends and the jeweller's work begins: the item is brought to the shape you want, one affix at a time."]},
            "tier_haven": {"title": "Haven", "text": [
                "Apotheosis divides the world into five tiers, and CTRL+T shows the current one. Haven is the first: only grey and green drop, rare slips through about once in twenty-five finds, and epic and mythic do not exist at all.",
                "The tier opens on its own the moment you hold your first affixed item."]},
            "tier_frontier": {"title": "Frontier", "text": [
                "Frontier asks for a full set of affixed gear: helmet, chestplate, leggings, boots and a weapon, all affixed. In return green becomes the standard find and epic stops being impossible.",
                "From this tier the overworld gems start appearing: the Earth, the Royal Family, the Verdant Ruin."]},
            "tier_ascent": {"title": "Ascent", "text": [
                "A full set of uncommon gear and the head of an Apothic Invader open the third tier. Rare becomes the most common find, and the Nether grows its own gems: the Ravenous Blood Lord and the Incandescent Inferno."]},
            "tier_summit": {"title": "Summit", "text": [
                "A full set of rare gear and a slain Wither lift the world to the second-to-last tier. Grey vanishes from the tables entirely, epic drops more often than everything else combined, and the End starts yielding Endersurge gems."]},
            "tier_pinnacle": {"title": "Pinnacle", "text": [
                "The last tier costs a full set of epic gear and the head of the Ender Dragon. After it, common, uncommon and all the low-end rubbish disappear from the tables: only rare, epic and mythic remain, and mythic is every fourth find.",
                "There is nowhere left to climb. All that remains is cutting gems to perfection and reforging the same gear until it comes out flawless."]},
        },
    },
}
